steps.text:
Heavy resistance exercise is different. With a thin tube (catheter) in the arm artery, five experienced bodybuilders lifting to failure reached a mean of **320/250 mmHg** in the double-leg press; one went above 480/350. Even a single-arm curl to failure gave 255/190. Pressures shot up as each weight was lifted and fell as it was lowered [[c:bp-lifting-peaks]].

Three things add up [[c:bp-lifting-causes]]:

- contracting muscle **squeezes its own vessels**, raising resistance;
- a strong pressor response;
- the [Valsalva manoeuvre](g:valsalva): straining against a closed throat raises chest pressure, which adds to arterial pressure.

So, unlike running or cycling, both systolic and diastolic soar, if only for seconds at a time.
